  "summary": "Auburn, CA's City Council voted 5-0 to delay a decision on its 12 Flock license-plate reader cameras, opting to survey residents and gather more information amid public pushback over data sharing. A recent audit found Auburn's Flock data had been accessible to out-of-state and federal agencies due to old settings, which police say have since been disabled. The council is weighing a standalone ALPR policy and possible community oversight committee.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "Auburn's City Council voted 5-0 Monday to delay a decision on whether to keep using the city's 12 Flock Safety license-plate reader cameras, choosing instead to gather more information and survey residents after weeks of public pushback over how the footage has been shared.",
      "paragraph_idx": 6
    },
    {
      "quote": "A recent audit found that Auburn Flock data had been accessible in some cases to out-of-state and federal agencies; police said that access has since been shut off",
      "paragraph_idx": 10
    },
    {
      "quote": "Auburn has no official city policy governing automated license plate readers; the police department's policy serves as the default",
      "paragraph_idx": 16
    },
    {
      "quote": "If Auburn ends the contract early, it would owe at least $6,000\u2014$500 for each of its 12 cameras",
      "paragraph_idx": 16
    }
  ],
